                "Enacted by the United States Congress on July 30, 2002, the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 (Pub.L. 107–204, commonly abbreviated as SOX) represents the most far-reaching statutory reform of corporate securities law and financial reporting since the Great Depression. Authored by Senator Paul Sarbanes and Representative Michael Oxley in the direct aftermath of Enron and WorldCom, SOX aimed to restore public confidence in corporate disclosures, establish criminal accountability for corporate fraud, create an independent accounting oversight board (the PCAOB), and impose strict mandates on executive management and independent auditors.",
                "Section 302: Corporate Responsibility for Financial Reports: Section 302 of SOX establishes direct, personal legal accountability for executive leadership. Prior to SOX, corporate executives facing fraud indictments frequently claimed ignorance, arguing that accounting fabrications were buried deep within operational sub-ledgers without their knowledge. Section 302 permanently dismantled this defense. Under Section 302 and SEC Rule 13a-14, the Principal Executive Officer (CEO) and Principal Financial Officer (CFO) must personally sign a legally binding certification included in every quarterly (Form 10-Q) and annual (Form 10-K) report.",
                "In their Section 302 certification, the CEO and CFO certify under oath that: First, they have reviewed the report; Second, based on their knowledge, the report does not contain any untrue statement of a material fact or omit a material fact necessary to make the statements not misleading; Third, based on their knowledge, the financial statements fairly present in all material respects the financial condition and results of operations; Fourth, they are responsible for establishing and maintaining Disclosure Controls and Procedures (DCP) and Internal Control Over Financial Reporting (ICFR); Fifth, they have designed controls to ensure material information is made known to them; and Sixth, they have evaluated control effectiveness within the preceding 90 days and disclosed all significant deficiencies, material weaknesses, and any fraud involving management or key employees to the audit committee and external auditors.",
