                "Exclusive Statutory Mandates of the Audit Committee: SOX Section 301 permanently severed the traditional reporting line between external auditors and executive management. The Audit Committee possesses exclusive, non-delegable authority over: First, The Appointment, Compensation, and Oversight of External Auditors: The audit committee is directly responsible for hiring, firing, establishing audit fees, and overseeing the work of the registered public accounting firm. The external auditor reports directly to the audit committee, not to the CEO or CFO; Second, Pre-Approval of All Services: The audit committee must pre-approve all audit services and permissible non-audit services (such as tax compliance), strictly barring prohibited non-audit services (such as bookkeeping, financial information systems design, or internal audit outsourcing under SOX Section 201); Third, Whistleblower Procedures: Under Section 301 and Section 806, the audit committee must establish confidential, anonymous mechanisms for the receipt, retention, and treatment of complaints regarding accounting, internal accounting controls, or auditing matters; and Fourth, Engaging Independent Advisors: The audit committee has independent statutory authority to engage separate legal counsel and financial advisors at the company's expense without requiring executive approval.",
