                "The Architecture of the Three Lines Model: The IIA model organizes governance and risk oversight into three structural tiers accountable to the governing body (Board of Directors and Audit Committee): First Line Roles (Operational Management): Operational executives and line management who directly lead and manage operational activities, design day-to-day workflow processes, and own the frontline internal controls that mitigate operational, compliance, and financial reporting risks; Second Line Roles (Risk Management, Compliance, Internal Controls): Specialized corporate functions that provide expertise, support, monitoring, and constructive challenge to frontline management, establishing risk management frameworks, monitoring regulatory compliance, and overseeing SOX internal control testing programs; Third Line Roles (Internal Audit): An independent, objective internal assurance and consulting activity that provides the governing body and executive management with comprehensive assurance on the adequacy, effectiveness, and efficiency of governance, risk management, and internal controls.",
                "Structural Independence of the Third Line: To maintain unassailable objectivity, the Chief Audit Executive (CAE) and the internal audit function must be structurally insulated from operational line management. In best-practice governance, the CAE maintains a dual reporting relationship: a direct functional reporting line to the Board Audit Committee (for charter approval, audit plan authorization, budget, and performance evaluation), and an administrative reporting line to the Chief Executive Officer (for day-to-day administrative coordination). Internal audit is strictly prohibited from assuming operational management responsibilities, because an auditor cannot objectively audit their own prior operational decisions.",
                "Statutory External Audit Standards: ISA 610 (Revised) and PCAOB AS 2605: When the independent external auditor plans the statutory financial statement and SOX 404 audit, can the external auditor rely on the work performed by internal audit? Under International Standard on Auditing 610 (Revised), 'Using the Work of Internal Auditors,' and PCAOB Auditing Standard AS 2605, 'Consideration of the Internal Audit Function,' the external auditor is permitted to utilize internal audit work to reduce substantive testing or obtain direct assistance, subject to strict professional protocols.",
                "The Three Evaluative Criteria under ISA 610: Before using the work of the internal audit function, the external auditor must systematically evaluate: First, Objectivity: The organizational status of internal audit, including whether the function reports directly to the audit committee, whether internal auditors are free from conflicting operational duties, and whether compensation is tied to company financial performance; Second, Competence: Whether internal audit personnel possess professional certifications (such as Certified Internal Auditor [CIA] or CPA), technical training, and adequate resources; and Third, Systematic and Disciplined Approach: Whether the function employs rigorous audit methodologies, quality control procedures, and follows the International Professional Practices Framework (IPPF).",
